1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,600
Your coding agents are capable.

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:05,300
But every session… starts from zero.

3
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:07,550
They re-read the same files.

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:10,081
They retry the fix your team already reverted.

5
00:00:10,131 --> 00:00:12,454
After compaction, they lose their place.

6
00:00:12,554 --> 00:00:15,444
And every re-read? You pay for it, in tokens.

7
00:00:15,844 --> 00:00:19,360
This is Cairn: institutional memory for coding agents.

8
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:23,960
Five layers: map, specs, timeline, memory and sessions.

9
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:28,097
Before an edit, your agent asks Cairn: impact, why, context.

10
00:00:28,247 --> 00:00:31,353
And it gets a cited answer, inside a token budget.

11
00:00:31,753 --> 00:00:36,628
On Cairn's own repo, one answer took… 391 tokens.

12
00:00:36,828 --> 00:00:42,705
Not 144,228 tokens, across 33 files.

13
00:00:43,155 --> 00:00:46,755
369 times fewer.

14
00:00:47,055 --> 00:00:51,655
And after compaction, the next session picks up where it left off.

15
00:00:51,905 --> 00:00:55,844
One command, in any git repo. Local. No API key.

16
00:00:55,994 --> 00:01:00,515
Works with Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini, Copilot and OpenCode.

17
00:01:00,965 --> 00:01:05,165
Cairn. Memory your agents can cite.
